cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this corpus, please cite it as below. Use the concept DOI to refer to the corpus in general (it resolves to the latest release) and the version DOI to refer to exactly the release you used."
type: dataset
title: "gpconf: a conformance corpus for orbital-data parsers crossing the five-digit catalog-number boundary"
version: "0.6.2"
date-released: "2026-10-09"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22867654
license: MIT
authors:
  - given-names: Honorius
    family-names: Neogy
    affiliation: "NEOGY LLC"
    # role: author and maintainer; copyright holder is NEOGY LLC
repository-code: "https://github.com/hneogy/gp-omm-conformance"
abstract: >-
  Expected values, structural checks, specification vectors, derived Alpha-5 TLE sets and a
  standard-library runner that test whether an orbital-data parser handles the 2026 migration of
  the US satellite catalog past five-digit numbers: Alpha-5 encoding, six- and nine-digit catalog
  numbers, and the CCSDS Orbit Mean-Elements Message in CSV, JSON, XML and KVN as served by
  CelesTrak. No provider data is redistributed, and one provider response is, the 16-byte answer
  "No GP data found"; a fetch script rebuilds the data locally under the provider's terms and
  SHA-256 hashes identify the tested snapshot. Built with AI assistance under
  human direction; v0.1.0 was audited by a separate AI session with no access to the build context
  (AUDIT.md); decision log in DECISIONS.md.
keywords:
  - satellite catalog
  - two-line element set
  - Alpha-5
  - OMM
  - CCSDS 502.0-B-3
  - SGP4
  - CelesTrak
  - conformance testing
